WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.483 --> 00:00:06.525
Marcus Bell: Hey Dana. Is Jeff joining us?

2
00:00:06.966 --> 00:00:16.961
Dana Ortiz: He's here, he's just in the other room finishing up a call with one of the branches. He'll pop on in a sec.

3
00:00:17.179 --> 00:00:23.051
Marcus Bell: No rush. How is Charlotte? I saw you guys got hammered with rain last week.

4
00:00:23.608 --> 00:00:33.151
Dana Ortiz: Oh, it was a mess. We had, I want to say, three days straight. Which, honestly, for us is not the worst thing.

5
00:00:33.151 --> 00:00:37.715
Dana Ortiz: Water heaters, sump pumps, the phones were ringing off the hook.

6
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.775
Marcus Bell: Right, one person's bad week is a great week for plumbing.

7
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:51.354
Dana Ortiz: Exactly. Our dispatch team was not thrilled with me when I said that out loud, though.

8
00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:58.362
Jeff Kim: Okay, sorry, sorry, I'm here. The Gastonia branch had a van break down, so that was fun.

9
00:00:59.457 --> 00:01:02.411
Marcus Bell: Hey Jeff. No worries at all. Everything okay?

10
00:01:03.644 --> 00:01:11.125
Jeff Kim: Yeah, yeah, we got it towed, we moved two jobs to the Belmont crew. Just a Tuesday. Well, a Wednesday.

11
00:01:12.102 --> 00:01:34.252
Marcus Bell: Ha. Okay, so I put an agenda in the invite, but it's pretty loose. I wanted to go through the Q3 numbers, talk about where you're at with Signal AI and the summaries rollout, and then, since we're about twelve weeks out from renewal, just get a sense of how you're both feeling going into that.

12
00:01:34.252 --> 00:01:35.439
Marcus Bell: Does that work?

13
00:01:35.652 --> 00:01:40.906
Dana Ortiz: That works. I have a hard stop at eleven, just so you know.

14
00:01:42.024 --> 00:01:53.444
Marcus Bell: Totally. Quick numbers, let me pull it up. Okay. So Q3, you had about forty-one thousand inbound calls across the fourteen branches, which is up eleven percent over Q2.

15
00:01:53.444 --> 00:02:06.045
Marcus Bell: Paid search is still your biggest source, a little over half. And the booked-job rate on paid search calls was thirty-eight percent, which is actually your best quarter since you came on.

16
00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:10.137
Jeff Kim: Is that across all fourteen, or is Belmont skewing it again?

17
00:02:10.965 --> 00:02:20.230
Marcus Bell: Let me look. Um, Belmont is high, they're at forty-four percent, but even if you take them out you're at thirty-seven.

18
00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:34.348
Marcus Bell: The one that's lagging is Matthews, they're at twenty-nine, and that's mostly because their after-hours calls go to the answering service and a lot of those don't convert until the next morning.

19
00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:44.170
Jeff Kim: Yeah, Matthews is the one branch we still roll to the answering service after six. We keep talking about bringing that in-house.

20
00:02:44.439 --> 00:02:54.616
Marcus Bell: If you do, it's a small change on our side. We'd just update the call flow so after-hours calls for Matthews ring your central dispatch line instead.

21
00:02:54.616 --> 00:02:56.123
Marcus Bell: Takes a few minutes.

22
00:02:56.655 --> 00:03:02.626
Jeff Kim: Okay, good to know. I'll talk to Carla about it, she'd have to staff it.

23
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:13.312
Marcus Bell: And then Meta is still small for you, right around six percent of calls, but the booked rate there is decent, thirty-one percent.

24
00:03:13.312 --> 00:03:14.577
Marcus Bell: So not nothing.

25
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:24.398
Dana Ortiz: Yeah, we're testing more on Meta in Q1. We'll see. Our agency wants to push it, I'm a little skeptical.

26
00:03:25.447 --> 00:03:32.279
Dana Ortiz: But anyway, overall, that is good to hear, because it does not feel like our best quarter.

27
00:03:32.848 --> 00:03:34.179
Marcus Bell: Okay. Tell me more.

28
00:03:34.841 --> 00:03:43.629
Dana Ortiz: So this is the thing I really wanted to get into today. The calls are coming in, the bookings are fine.

29
00:03:43.629 --> 00:03:57.439
Dana Ortiz: But on the Google Ads side, the offline conversions are showing up two, sometimes three days after the call. And it's not every now and then, it's been most of September and October.

30
00:03:58.797 --> 00:04:05.808
Marcus Bell: Two to three days from when the call happens to when it shows up as a conversion in Google Ads?

31
00:04:06.114 --> 00:04:09.341
Dana Ortiz: Right. And so what happens is Smart Bidding

32
00:04:09.867 --> 00:04:18.741
Jeff Kim: Sorry, can I, just so Marcus has the full picture. It's the booked job signal specifically, right? Not the call itself?

33
00:04:19.705 --> 00:04:33.108
Dana Ortiz: Yeah, the booked job one, that's the one we bid on. Anyway, so Smart Bidding sees a day where we spent, whatever, nine grand and got barely any conversions, and it panics and pulls back.

34
00:04:33.108 --> 00:04:43.830
Dana Ortiz: And then three days later all the conversions land and it goes, oh, never mind, and swings the other way. So we're basically bidding on last week's data.

35
00:04:45.036 --> 00:04:49.347
Marcus Bell: Got it. Okay. Yeah, that would make the account look really choppy.

36
00:04:49.788 --> 00:05:03.617
Dana Ortiz: Choppy is a nice word for it. Our CPA in Google went from about eighty-five dollars to a hundred and twenty in like five weeks, and my CMO keeps asking me why.

37
00:05:03.617 --> 00:05:10.099
Dana Ortiz: And I don't have a good answer for him other than the conversions are late.

38
00:05:10.368 --> 00:05:19.034
Marcus Bell: Did anything change on your side around then? New conversion actions, the GCLID capture, anything with the tag on the site?

39
00:05:19.377 --> 00:05:22.803
Dana Ortiz: Not that I know of. Jeff, did Priyanka change anything?

40
00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:30.204
Jeff Kim: No, she checked. The conversion actions are the same ones from last year. Nothing changed on our end.

41
00:05:31.304 --> 00:05:44.685
Marcus Bell: Okay. I'll open a ticket with our integrations team today, and I'll ask them to pull the upload logs for your account so we can see when we're sending versus when Google is accepting them.

42
00:05:44.685 --> 00:05:53.096
Marcus Bell: I've heard something similar from at least one other customer this month, so I want to see if it's the same thing.

43
00:05:54.412 --> 00:06:00.474
Dana Ortiz: Please. Because I'll be honest with you, this is the one that keeps me up at night.

44
00:06:00.746 --> 00:06:13.072
Jeff Kim: And the other piece, Marcus, I'll just say it. CallRail's been emailing me, like, every week. Their rep keeps telling me they push conversions to Google same day.

45
00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:23.198
Jeff Kim: I don't know if that's true, I haven't looked into it, but it's hard to ignore when we're sitting here three days behind.

46
00:06:24.063 --> 00:06:32.740
Marcus Bell: Yeah. That's fair, and I appreciate you telling me. I'd want to see what same day actually means for them, there's usually some fine print.

47
00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:37.252
Marcus Bell: But that's not a reason for us to be three days late. Understood.

48
00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:44.398
Jeff Kim: Yeah. I'm not shopping. I'm just saying the emails are getting harder to delete.

49
00:06:45.027 --> 00:06:50.876
Marcus Bell: Ha, fair enough. Okay, let's talk Signal AI, because I know you've been living in there, Jeff.

50
00:06:51.690 --> 00:07:03.164
Jeff Kim: Yeah, so, mostly good. The one thing, and I sent Priyanka down a rabbit hole on this, Signal AI is tagging a lot of our after-hours calls as spam, and they're not spam.

51
00:07:03.164 --> 00:07:07.684
Jeff Kim: They're people with a burst pipe at eleven at night. They're booking jobs.

52
00:07:08.467 --> 00:07:11.285
Marcus Bell: Huh. How many are we talking about?

53
00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:20.908
Jeff Kim: So we pulled forty of the after-hours calls it marked as spam last week and actually listened to them, and thirty-one were real customers.

54
00:07:20.908 --> 00:07:24.343
Jeff Kim: Real jobs. A couple of them were four-hundred-dollar emergency calls.

55
00:07:24.551 --> 00:07:28.055
Marcus Bell: Thirty-one out of forty. Okay, that's a lot.

56
00:07:28.588 --> 00:07:40.588
Jeff Kim: It is. And the problem is the spam ones drop out of the reporting, so the after-hours campaign looks terrible, and then somebody says, hey, why are we paying for after-hours ads.

57
00:07:41.365 --> 00:07:43.939
Dana Ortiz: Which is me. I'm the somebody.

58
00:07:45.008 --> 00:07:46.035
Jeff Kim: She's the somebody.

59
00:07:46.564 --> 00:07:50.124
Marcus Bell: Is it overnight calls specifically, or anything outside business hours?

60
00:07:50.704 --> 00:08:00.565
Jeff Kim: It seems like it's mostly after about nine p.m., and weekends. Short calls, people who are kind of panicked and just give their address and hang up.

61
00:08:00.565 --> 00:08:03.852
Jeff Kim: Those seem to get flagged. But that's me guessing.

62
00:08:04.811 --> 00:08:12.918
Marcus Bell: No, that's helpful. My guess is the spam model is leaning on call length, and maybe on the time of day.

63
00:08:12.918 --> 00:08:20.254
Marcus Bell: I'll send this over to the Signal AI team with your examples if you can share the call IDs.

64
00:08:21.435 --> 00:08:24.215
Jeff Kim: I'll send you the spreadsheet. Priyanka has it.

65
00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:30.458
Marcus Bell: Perfect. And the summaries, how is that going? You turned those on for dispatch in, what, August?

66
00:08:30.991 --> 00:08:46.981
Jeff Kim: Oh, the summaries. Honestly, the dispatchers love them. Like, genuinely. Carla told me they used to have to listen to the first minute of every call to figure out what the person even wanted, and now they just read the summary and go.

67
00:08:47.938 --> 00:08:50.557
Marcus Bell: That's great. That's really good to hear.

68
00:08:51.135 --> 00:08:56.402
Jeff Kim: It probably saves each of them an hour a day, I'd guess. She'd say more.

69
00:08:57.597 --> 00:09:00.530
Dana Ortiz: She would say two hours. She exaggerates.

70
00:09:01.658 --> 00:09:08.041
Marcus Bell: I'll take an hour. Um, and the branch managers, are they reading them too, or is it just dispatch?

71
00:09:08.845 --> 00:09:17.910
Jeff Kim: Mostly dispatch for now. A couple of the managers check them when there's a complaint, but we haven't really pushed it.

72
00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:27.161
Marcus Bell: Okay, that's maybe something we look at next quarter. Oh, and Jeff, how was the trade show? You were in Orlando, right?

73
00:09:28.374 --> 00:09:33.781
Jeff Kim: Orlando, yeah. It was fine. A lot of booths, a lot of software people. No offense.

74
00:09:34.142 --> 00:09:36.874
Marcus Bell: None taken. I am a software people.

75
00:09:37.458 --> 00:09:48.249
Jeff Kim: Ha. Honestly the best part was a panel on technician retention. Everybody's got the same problem, nobody wants to crawl under a house in August.

76
00:09:49.268 --> 00:09:50.340
Dana Ortiz: Can't imagine why.

77
00:09:51.093 --> 00:10:01.025
Marcus Bell: Ha. Okay, so Dana, you mentioned renewal. I know we're still a few months out, but I'd rather hear it now than in December.

78
00:10:01.803 --> 00:10:16.393
Dana Ortiz: Yeah. So a couple of things. Our finance team, Rob specifically, has been going through all of our software renewals for next year, and he asked me why PreSense is an add-on at all. His words were, it feels like paying twice.

79
00:10:16.393 --> 00:10:23.688
Dana Ortiz: Because we're already paying for the calls and the call data, and then PreSense is this separate line item on top.

80
00:10:24.381 --> 00:10:25.181
Marcus Bell: Okay.

81
00:10:25.868 --> 00:10:37.028
Dana Ortiz: And I tried to explain it. Like, no, PreSense is the part that passes what they were looking at on the site to the agent before they pick up.

82
00:10:37.028 --> 00:10:43.570
Dana Ortiz: And he said, okay, but isn't that just the call data? And I didn't love my answer.

83
00:10:43.898 --> 00:10:57.429
Marcus Bell: Yeah. I can put together a one-pager for Rob on what PreSense is doing for you specifically. We can look at the booked rate on calls where the agent had the page context versus calls where they didn't.

84
00:10:57.747 --> 00:11:04.226
Dana Ortiz: That would help. He's a numbers guy. If it's not a number, it doesn't exist.

85
00:11:05.274 --> 00:11:07.474
Marcus Bell: Got it. I can do numbers.

86
00:11:07.929 --> 00:11:17.351
Dana Ortiz: And then, I guess, the bigger thing. I don't want this to sound like a threat, because it's not. We like you guys.

87
00:11:17.351 --> 00:11:34.147
Dana Ortiz: But if the Google piece isn't fixed by renewal, we'll have to look around. I don't want to, but I'd have to, because I can't go to my CMO and say, yeah, we're renewing the thing that's making our CPA worse.

88
00:11:35.235 --> 00:11:52.394
Marcus Bell: No, that's completely fair, and I'd rather you tell me straight. I'll make sure the integrations ticket gets the right priority, and I'll give you an update by the end of next week either way, even if the update is that we're still digging.

89
00:11:53.088 --> 00:11:54.398
Dana Ortiz: Okay. I appreciate that.

90
00:11:55.854 --> 00:11:57.048
Jeff Kim: Yeah, that'd help.

91
00:11:58.328 --> 00:12:03.199
Marcus Bell: Anything else on your list? I think you had something about adding users.

92
00:12:03.499 --> 00:12:13.219
Jeff Kim: Oh, yeah, small thing. We're opening the two new branches in January, Rock Hill and Concord, so we'll need tracking numbers and a couple more seats.

93
00:12:13.219 --> 00:12:15.088
Jeff Kim: I'll send you Priyanka's list.

94
00:12:15.592 --> 00:12:26.209
Marcus Bell: Easy. I'll get the numbers provisioned once I have the list. Okay, so on my side: the integrations ticket on the offline conversion delay goes in today, with the upload logs.

95
00:12:26.209 --> 00:12:33.745
Marcus Bell: The after-hours spam examples go to the Signal AI team once Jeff sends the call IDs. A one-pager on PreSense for Rob.

96
00:12:33.745 --> 00:12:36.142
Marcus Bell: And the new branch numbers in January.

97
00:12:36.806 --> 00:12:37.606
Dana Ortiz: Perfect.

98
00:12:38.943 --> 00:12:42.671
Marcus Bell: Last thing, I promise. Are you guys doing anything for Thanksgiving?

99
00:12:42.886 --> 00:12:46.150
Dana Ortiz: My in-laws are coming. So, pray for me.

100
00:12:46.479 --> 00:12:50.638
Jeff Kim: We're doing the Panthers game. Well, my son is making me.

101
00:12:50.855 --> 00:12:56.396
Marcus Bell: Ha. Good luck with that. Okay, thanks, both of you. I'll send notes this afternoon.

102
00:12:57.088 --> 00:12:57.888
Dana Ortiz: Thanks, Marcus.

103
00:12:59.067 --> 00:12:59.867
Jeff Kim: Thanks, man.
